# Why is the ploughman bigger than the hero? Bruegel's Fall of Icarus
tags: ["renaissance painting","bruegel","composition and meaning","art history","perspective and scale","ordinary life in art"]
teaches: ["art-history/composition-analysis","concepts/visual-hierarchy","history/renaissance-values","methods/close-looking"]
thumb: "band \"Why is he bigger than Icarus?\" picture \"File:Pieter Bruegel the Elder - Landscape with the Fall of Icarus - Brussels, Royal Museums of Fine Arts of Belgium - Google Arts & Culture.jpg\" colour"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, unhurried gallery guide, quietly amused"}
prompt: "What does Bruegel put in front, and what in the corner? What does that say about whose life matters?"
next: {"title":"What does a day's work look like? Bruegel's The Harvesters","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/ordinary-life-takes-the-stage/02-what-does-a-day-s-work-look-like-bruegel.cast"}

## Why is the ploughman bigger than the hero?
sources:
    - id: rmfab
      title: Landscape with the Fall of Icarus (Google Arts & Culture story)
      authors: Royal Museums of Fine Arts of Belgium, Brussels
      year: 2024
      finding: Unsigned, undated, contested authorship, probably a faithful copy of a lost original; the ploughman, the shepherd gazing at the sky, the angler absorbed in his work; the proverb 'No plough will stop for a dying man'
      url: https://artsandculture.google.com/exhibit/landscape-with-the-fall-of-icarus/MgIyXpmuNdcLJg
    - id: wiki
      title: Landscape with the Fall of Icarus
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: c. 1560, Brussels; usually seen as an early copy of a lost Bruegel original of about 1558; ploughman, shepherd and angler from Ovid
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landscape_with_the_Fall_of_Icarus
    - id: ovid
      title: Metamorphoses, Book VIII
      authors: Ovid
      year: 8
      finding: An angler, a shepherd leaning on his crook and a ploughman see Daedalus and Icarus fly and stand amazed, taking them for gods

    card title "Landscape with the Fall of Icarus"

Who gets to be the subject of a great painting?
    image art cites ["rmfab","wiki"] credit "After Bruegel, c. 1560. Brussels. Public domain." x 500 y 335 width 900 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Pieter_Bruegel_the_Elder_-_Landscape_with_the_Fall_of_Icarus_-_Brussels%2C_Royal_Museums_of_Fine_Arts_of_Belgium_-_Google_Arts_%26_Culture.jpg/1920px-Pieter_Bruegel_the_Elder_-_Landscape_with_the_Fall_of_Icarus_-_Brussels%2C_Royal_Museums_of_Fine_Arts_of_Belgium_-_Google_Arts_%26_Culture.jpg look screen regions.angler [0.84,0.89,0.045,0.085] regions.corner [0.76,0.7,0.14,0.3] regions.furrows [0.18,0.8,0.36,0.18] regions.legs [0.835,0.722,0.042,0.052] regions.plough_team [0.08,0.53,0.41,0.33] regions.ploughman [0.365,0.535,0.13,0.35] regions.shepherd [0.495,0.52,0.04,0.15] regions.ship [0.68,0.41,0.22,0.26] regions.sky [0,0,1,0.25]

This course follows that questi (@highlight art:ploughman strength soft@)on over three centuries, and it starts here, around 1560.

The design is by Pieter Bruegel, a Flemish painter;
    portrait bruegel x 905 y 500 width 140 of "Pieter Bruegel the Elder" cameo true

this canvas in Brussels is probably an early copy of his lost original.
    erase bruegel

Its title promises a myth: Icarus, who flew with his father Daedalus on wings of feathers and wax, went too close to the sun, and fell into the sea.
    highlight art:sky lift true

But the biggest figure in the picture is  (@highlight art:ploughman lift true@)no hero. It's a farmer, ploughing.

    camera on ["art@[0.06,0.46,0.46,0.54]"]

He gets the front of the stage, a bright red shirt, and the whole foreground for his work.
    highlight art:plough_team

Bruegel paints each ridge of turned earth, curving with the slope of the field.
    highlight art:furrows lift true

    camera reset true

The ploughman comes from the myth itself: the Roman poet Ovid says a ploughman, a  (@highlight art:ploughman art:shepherd lift true@)shepherd and a fisherman, still hidden, saw the flight.

    quiz "What do Ovid's three do when they see them fly?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 500 buttons_at.y 500
        intro: In Ovid's poem, what do those three do when they see father and son fly overhead?
        right: They stop and stare, taking father and son for gods.
        wrong: In a myth, a boy flying across the sky is not something you ignore.
        + Stare up in wonder
        * Keep on working
        * Kneel and pray

Bruegel turns that around. His ploughman keeps his eyes on the furrow.
    highlight art:ploughman lift true

    camera on ["art@[0.36,0.12,0.3,0.55]"]

The shepherd, leaning on his staff, does look up,
    highlight art:shepherd lift true

but at the empty sky, away from the sea.
    point at.ref art@[0.47,0.2,0.04,0.05] gesture glow

    camera reset true

    ask "Find the fisherman. Tap him." spot angler on art
        intro: Ovid's third witness is here too.
        right: There: an angler on the rocks, watching his line.
        wrong: Look low down on the right, at the water's edge.

    camera on ["art:corner"]

And the fisherman sits closest of all to the myth.
    highlight art:angler lift true

Right beside him, tw (@highlight art:legs strength strong rest grey@)o legs are kicking in the water. That's Icarus.

    camera reset true

The hero of the title, a splash in the corner,
    highlight art:legs lift true

while a ship sails on past him.
    highlight art:ship lift true

So the sizes are flipped: the farmer is huge,
    highlight art:ploughman lift true

the myth is small.
    highlight art:legs lift true

Some read the ploughman through an old saying: no plough stops for a dying man.
    highlight art:plough_team lift true

    camera on ["art@[0.06,0.46,0.46,0.54]"]

Read one way, that's a cruel picture: nobody cares. Read another way (@highlight art:plough_team rest paper lift true@), it's a new idea of what counts: a working day, painted with the care once kept for gods and heroes.

    camera reset true

    quiz "A painter today wants to show a royal wedding the way Bruegel shows Icarus. Which picture follows his method?"
        right: The sweeper in front: the working day fills the stage, and the famous event shrinks to a corner.
        wrong: In Bruegel, who is big, and where is the famous event?
        * The couple huge, a sweeper tiny below
        * A crowd of guests filling the frame
        + A sweeper in front, the balcony tiny behind
        * The balcony close up, sweeper cut off

The ploughman pushed a (@highlight art:legs lift true@) myth into the corner.

In this course, ordinary  (@highlight art:ploughman lift true@)life keeps walking towards the centre.

